  "question": {
    "text": "If <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> has 'X' number of unpaired electrons and 'Y' other positive oxidation states than +2. What is the sum of X + Y here?"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "10",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>→</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></math><br /><br />unpaired <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math><br /><br />Mn can show +3, +4, +5, +6 and +7 oxidation states also other than +2.<br /><br />Y = 5<br /><br />X + Y = 5 + 5 = 10"
  }
}